  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "ozV79u/5kQCbipPFF3wqu/kLr4I=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:23.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## Throwing is wrong for collections\n\nA resolver that returns *one* document should fail closed and throw. A resolver that returns *many* must not: throwing on the first forbidden document turns one protected item into a total failure for a caller who was entitled to the rest of the list. Collection resolvers **drop** what the caller cannot read and return the remainder.\n\nThe shape has two parts \u2014 a policy-wide short circuit, then a per-item non-throwing check:\n\n```typescript\nimport type { CanonicalDocumentId } from \"@powerhousedao/reactor-api\";\n\nsearchTodos: async (\n  _parent,\n  args: { driveId: string; searchTerm: string },\n  ctx: Context,\n) => {\n  const matches = await runSearch(args.driveId, args.searchTerm); // document ids\n\n  // Policy-wide callers see everything; skip the per-item check.\n  if (subgraph.authorizationService.isSupremeAdmin(ctx.user?.address)) {\n    return matches;\n  }\n\n  const visible: string[] = [];\n  for (const id of matches) {\n    // non-throwing; ids returned by the Reactor are already canonical\n    if (await subgraph.canReadDocument(id as CanonicalDocumentId, ctx)) {\n      visible.push(id);\n    }\n  }\n  return visible;\n};\n```\n\n## Why filtering, not post-hoc shaping\n\nFiltering **on read** means the resolver never leaks the *existence* of a protected document through a list endpoint. That is a stronger property than redacting fields after the fetch: an id in a search result is itself information, and a caller who can enumerate ids can probe them individually.\n\nThe `isSupremeAdmin` short circuit is a performance concession, not a correctness one \u2014 supreme admins and the `OPEN` policy would pass every per-item check anyway, so the loop is pure overhead for them.\n\nThis is the same shape the built-in `findDocuments` and relationship resolvers use. The corresponding best practice is stated plainly: filter lists, don't throw on them; reserve the throwing asserts for single-target resolvers.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/03-BuildingUserExperiences/07-Authorization/05-EnforcingAuthorizationInSubgraphs.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:23.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:23.000Z"
    }
  },
